riety level</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Bishinga</td><td align="left">Cultivated1 (edible) sorghum</td><td align="left">Muyra</td><td align="left"><italic>Muyra Red</italic></td><td align="left"><italic>Muyra red long sweet stalked</italic></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td align="left"><italic>Muyra Brown</italic></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td align="left"><italic>Muyra Yellow</italic></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td align="left"><italic>Muyra Dark Red</italic></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">Wild<sup>2 </sup>(non-edible) sorghum</td><td align="left"><italic>Degengof</italic></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td align="left"><italic>Keelo (Harchatee)</italic></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td align="left"><italic>Firekolef</italic></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p><underline>Legend:</underline><sup>1</sup><bold>edible sorghum</bold>-sorghum used for food, feed, fuel wood and construction and are cultivated, selected and maintained by the farmers</p><p><sup>2</sup><bold>non-edible sorghum</bold>-sorghum with no common uses and are not grown, selected and maintained by the farmers but are self-grown or -propagated on the farm without farmers knowledge or selection</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T4"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Farmers' criteria for separating wild and cultivated types</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Characters for comparison</td><td align="left">Wild (non-edible) types <bold>(e.g. <italic>Harchatee</italic>)</bold></td><td align="left">Cultivated (edible) types <bold>(e.g. <italic>Muyra</italic>)</bold></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Panicle type</td><td align="left">Lax</td><td align="left">Compact to semi-compact</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Glume colour and coverage</td><td align="left">Full</td><t